# TaskManager — optional install-time privilege widening (no runtime sudo).
#
# The app itself never carries setcap/setuid and never prompts on its own
# unless the user enables a per-feature escalation (polkit helpers, ADR-023).
# These udev rules use the same mechanism Mission Center's setup script does:
# the udev daemon (running as root) widens the mode ONCE at device appearance,
# so every later unprivileged launch reads the data without privileges.
#
# Install (systemd systems):
#   sudo install -Dm644 99-taskforest.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/99-taskforest.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# What each rule opens and why:
#   * intel-rapl energy_uj — CPU package power draw (RAPL). The kernel ships it
#     0400 root; without this rule the app reports typed PermissionDenied for
#     CPU power on Intel hosts (never a fabricated 0 W).
#
# NOT included by design:
#   * /dev/nvme* / /dev/sd* (SMART) — device-node access is a distribution
#     policy (usually 0660 root:disk); add your user to the `disk` group or
#     write your own rule. SMART stays a typed PermissionDenied otherwise.
#   * perf_event (Intel per-engine %) — the app escalates per-feature via its
#     polkit helper instead of a blanket capability grant.
#   * AF_PACKET (per-process network) — the app escalates per-feature via its
#     polkit net-launcher; a setcap on a dedicated helper binary is a future
#     design option, never on the main binary.
SUBSYSTEM=="powercap", KERNEL=="intel-rapl*", RUN+="/usr/bin/chmod a+r /sys/%p/energy_uj"
